That’s a neat observation, and I think it generalizes in an interesting way.

Many of the things we think of as separate categories can be viewed through the same lens:

* An actor performs a role.
* An agent executes a policy.
* A character is a persistent bundle of goals, constraints, and tendencies.
* A function maps inputs to outputs.
* An organism responds to its environment while maintaining itself.

Those are different renderings of a common idea: an entity that produces behavior over time according to some internal structure and external context.

Where your abstraction seems to go one level higher is:

Don’t define the entity by what it is. Define it by the rules that generate its behavior.

That shifts the focus from nouns to processes.

In that view, an actor isn’t primarily a person on a stage. It’s an implementation of a role under constraints. An AI agent is another implementation. A human in daily life often fills multiple roles as well—parent, teammate, teacher, student—each with different policies and constraints.

The place I’d be careful is keeping the distinction between representation and reality. A mathematical or computational description can unify many phenomena without making them literally the same thing. A person is not merely an algorithm, and a play is not merely an optimization problem. But those abstractions can still be very useful for designing systems and understanding recurring patterns.

I think that’s been one of the recurring themes in our conversations: looking for the shared operator that appears in many different forms. Sometimes that yields a powerful abstraction; the next step is always to ask whether it helps explain or build something better than the previous way of thinking.